{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>עָזִּי וְזִמְרָת יָהּ וַיְהִי לִי לִישׁוּעָה.</b> These verses demonstrate that the righteous turn the attribute of judgment into mercy, while the wicked turn the attribute of mercy into judgment. This is what it says: ״עָזִּי״ — 'עֹז' means the force and strength of the attribute of judgment, as it is written (Psalms 21:2) ״ה׳ בְּעָזְּךָ יִשְׂמַח מֶלֶךְ״ — even in the attribute of judgment, which is called עֹז, the righteous king nonetheless rejoices in it, for joy for the righteous is to perform the true judgments of HaShem; ״וּבִישׁוּעָתְךָ״ — that is, the attribute of mercy — ״מַה יָּגֶל מְאֹד״. ״וְזִמְרָת״ is a term of cutting, from the language of ״כַּרְמְךָ לֹא תִזְמֹר״, and this too indicates the attribute of judgment. And the name יָהּ likewise indicates the attribute of judgment, as is explained in Rabbeinu Bachaye on the verse ״כִּי יָד עַל כֵּס יָהּ מִלְחָמָה לַה׳ בַּעֲמָלֵק״ (Exodus 17:16). And all these terms that trace back to the attribute of judgment — nevertheless ״וַיְהִי לִי לִישׁוּעָה״, they were turned for me into mercy and deliverance. And this is the precise force of the added vav of וַיְהִי. And through judgment the Holy One, blessed be He, does kindness with me, measure for measure, for I too bless HaShem for the bad just as I bless Him for the good; and even though the Egyptians did evil to us and to our fathers, nevertheless I exalt His name, blessed be He, over all. This is what it says: ״זֶה אֵלִי וְאַנְוֵהוּ אֱלֹהֵי אָבִי וַאֲרֹמְמֶנְהוּ״. ״אֵלִי״ and ״אֱלֹהֵי״ are all an allusion to the attribute of judgment, for ״אֵל״ is a term of strength and force; and even though Rashi interpreted in parashat Ki Tissa (Exodus 34:6) that ״אֵל״ is the attribute of mercy, and his proof is from the verse (Psalms 22:2) ״אֵלִי אֵלִי לָמָה עֲזַבְתָּנִי״, that proof is nothing — for behold, the righteous turn judgment into mercy, therefore he asked that even אֵל not abandon him. And the essential meaning of the term is a term of power, as in ״וְאֵת אֵילֵי הָאָרֶץ לָקָח״ (Ezekiel 17:13). This is what it says: ״זֶה אֵלִי״ — even though He deals with me by the attribute of judgment, nevertheless ״אַנְוֵהוּ״ — I shall proclaim His beauty and praise. And likewise ״אֱלֹהֵי אָבִי״ — even though the Egyptians did evil to our fathers too, nevertheless ״וַאֲרֹמְמֶנְהוּ״ — I shall exalt Him for this." "And if you wish, you may settle this according to Rashi's interpretation, that ״אֵלִי״ is the attribute of mercy; you would explain it thus: ״זֶה אֵלִי״ etc. — in the manner stated (Psalms 116:3-4) ״צָרָה וְיָגוֹן אֶמְצָא וּבְשֵׁם ה׳ אֶקְרָא, כּוֹס יְשׁוּעוֹת אֶשָּׂא וּבְשֵׁם ה׳ אֶקְרָא״, meaning: there is no distinction for me between trouble and sorrow and a cup of deliverances, for in both of them I call upon the name of HaShem to bless Him for both equally. So too he said: if ״זֶה אֵלִי״ and He deals with me by the attribute of mercy, then ״וְאַנְוֵהוּ״; and if He is ״אֱלֹהֵי אָבִי״ and deals with me by the attribute of judgment, as indicated by the name אֱלֹהִים, then ״וַאֲרֹמְמֶנְהוּ״ — for all is equal to me, whether judgment or mercy. Therefore the Holy One, blessed be He, measures me with this measure, to turn judgment into mercy for me. Not so the wicked, for on the contrary, they turn the attribute of mercy into judgment. This is what it says: ״ה׳ אִישׁ מִלְחָמָה ה׳ שְׁמוֹ״ (Exodus 15:3) — behold, His name ה׳, which indicates plain mercy, yet nevertheless He makes war against them until their downfall. And as it is written: ״וַיִּסַּע מַלְאַךְ הָאֱלֹהִים״ (Exodus 14:19) — that is the emissary of the attribute of judgment, and it traveled to deliver Israel. And it is written: ״וַיַּשְׁקֵף ה׳ אֶל מַחֲנֵה מִצְרַיִם בְּעַמּוּד אֵשׁ״ (Exodus 14:24) — ה׳ is the attribute of mercy, yet He looked upon them to strike them and destroy them. And all this is because they call the world to wage war against HaShem; but as for me, ״חֶסֶד וּמִשְׁפָּט אָשִׁירָה״ (Psalms 101:1) — whether for kindness or for judgment, I shall sing. And this is what it says: ״יְמִינְךָ ה׳ נֶאְדָּרִי בַּכֹּחַ, יְמִינְךָ ה׳ תִּרְעַץ אוֹיֵב״ (Exodus 15:6). Rashi explained: when Israel do the will of the Place, the left hand becomes the right hand, that is, the reversal of judgment into mercy. And one may explain further: ״יְמִינְךָ ה׳״ — that is, the right hand of HaShem that acts on the side of mercy with Israel — ״נֶאְדָּרִי בַּכֹּחַ״ already, meaning: the left hand, the attribute of severe judgment that acts with force and might, has already become a right hand for Israel. But the nations are not so; rather ״יְמִינְךָ ה׳״ — which was a right hand for the world — nevertheless ״תִּרְעַץ אוֹיֵב״, for it became a left hand against Pharaoh and his army."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 15, :notes {0 "The word אַנְוֵהוּ is translated here as 'I shall proclaim His beauty/praise' following the standard rabbinic derivation from נוֹי (beauty); the Kli Yakar's use of the phrase נוֹיוֹ וְשִׁבְחוֹ confirms this reading."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:20:37.455420943Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}